{
  "result": "There's a word I love: *sonder*.\n\nIt means the sudden realization that every random person passing you on the street is living a life as vivid and complex as your own.\n\nThat guy who cut you off in traffic this morning? He might have been rushing to the hospital because his wife is in labor. The barista who got your order wrong and seemed a little distracted? She was replaying an argument she had with her mom last night and trying not to cry. The kid on the subway with his headphones too loud, nodding his head slightly off-beat - in his head, he's the main character of an epic that you will never see.\n\nWe all walk around like we're the center of the movie, and everyone else is just an extra. But the truth is, there are eight billion main characters. Eight billion internal worlds, complete with private jokes, childhood scars, favorite songs they sing badly in the shower, and a very specific way they like their toast.\n\nI think about this a lot when I'm in airports. An airport is the best place to feel sonder. Look around the gate - a woman is coming home after two years abroad and her parents don't know she's arriving early. A man is flying to a city he's never been to for a job interview that could change his life. Two strangers are sharing fries and will never see each other again, but they'll both remember that conversation for a week.\n\nNone of them know each other. None of them know you. And yet for a moment, all your stories intersect in this one fluorescent-lit room with bad coffee.\n\nThe wild part is that most of those stories will stay invisible forever. You'll never know what the person next to you is carrying. That heaviness or lightness they have - you just see a face, a jacket, a pair of shoes.\n\nAnd maybe that's also what makes kindness so powerful. A small thing - letting someone go ahead of you in line, actually listening when someone talks, a genuine \"how are you?\" - it ripples out into a story you'll never fully know. You become a brief, kind footnote in someone else's epic without even realizing it.\n\nWe are all so temporary and so intertwined at the same time.\n\nIt's terrifying and beautiful, if you really sit with it.\n\n---\n\nWhat about you - when do you feel that feeling most? In crowds, late at night, or somewhere else entirely?",
